          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much advance should I pay a facade company?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "There is no single figure. Advances should be linked to defined stages such as design approval and material procurement, with later payments tied to dispatch, installation and handover. Seek legal or financial advice for large contracts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a red flag when hiring a facade contractor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pressure to pay quickly, no factory access, no written warranty, a lump-sum quote and a portfolio with no named projects are the most common warning signs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I choose the cheapest facade quote?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not automatically. Compare line by line. A cheaper quote may use thinner sheet, weaker coatings or leave out scaffolding and sealants, and the real cost shows up later."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a facade manufacturer better than a dealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturers control design, quality and timelines, which helps accountability. Dealers can be fine for simple products, but custom facades usually need a company that fabricates and installs its own work."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a mock-up for a small project?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, even a small one. A sample panel or small section lets you check colour, finish and joint lines under real light before you commit to full production."
